{
  "id": "artikel",
  "name": "Articles: a, an, the",
  "level": "A1",
  "subtitle": "Use a or an for one thing that is new or not special. Use the for a thing that we both know. A or an depends on the sound of the next word: a book, an apple.",
  "briefMarks": ["a or an", "the", "sound"],
  "source": "icanspeak Spanish original English text (grammar-a1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "a / an / the",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "+ noun",
        "variant": "prep"
      },
      {
        "text": "= which one?"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "In my garden there is <span class=\"vp-verb\">a</span> tree. <span class=\"vp-prep\">An</span> owl lives in <span class=\"vp-verb\">the</span> tree.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>a / an</strong> means one, and it is new or not special: <em>I have a dog. She is an engineer.</em> Use it only with a singular noun that you can count.",
        "tag": "a / an"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>a</strong> before a <em>consonant sound</em>, <strong>an</strong> before a <em>vowel sound</em>. Listen, do not look at the letter: <em>a university</em> (it starts with /j/), <em>an hour</em> (the h is silent).",
        "tag": "sound"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>the</strong> means we both know which one. Use it for the second time you talk about something (<em>I have a cat. The cat is black.</em>), for things there is only one of (<em>the sun, the moon</em>) and when it is clear from the situation (<em>Close the door, please.</em>).",
        "tag": "the"
      },
      {
        "html": "Use <strong>no article</strong> for plurals and uncountable nouns in general (<em>I like dogs. I like music.</em>), for meals (<em>We have lunch at one.</em>) and for most names (<em>Amira lives in Leeds.</em>).",
        "tag": "no article"
      },
      {
        "html": "A common mistake: <em>I am teacher.</em> Say <em>I am <strong>a</strong> teacher.</em> We use <em>a / an</em> with jobs. Another one: <em>a apple</em> → <em>an apple</em>.",
        "tag": "jobs"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Stories show the difference in two sentences: <em>Once upon a time there was <strong>a</strong> king. He had <strong>a</strong> daughter. <strong>The</strong> daughter was very happy.</em> First <em>a</em>, then <em>the</em>. Next: <a href=\"/nouns\">Nouns: people, things, places</a> and <a href=\"/plurals\">Plural nouns</a>.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide overview ▲",
