      "rule": {
        "label": "The sounds that decide: a or an",
        "groups": [
          {
            "artikel": "a",
            "endungen": [
              "b-",
              "c-",
              "d-",
              "m-",
              "s-",
              "u- = /juː/",
              "eu- = /juː/"
            ],
            "examples": "a book, a car, a university, a useful tool, a European city"
          },
          {
            "artikel": "an",
            "endungen": [
              "a-",
              "e-",
              "i-",
              "o-",
              "u- = /ʌ/",
              "silent h"
            ],
            "examples": "an apple, an egg, an umbrella, an hour, an honest man"
          },
          {
            "artikel": "the",
            "endungen": [
              "second mention",
              "only one",
              "clear from the situation"
            ],
            "examples": "the sun, the door, the cat from before"
          }
        ],
        "disclaimer": {
          "label": "Sound beats spelling: the exceptions",
          "html": "<p>The rule is about <strong>sounds</strong>, not letters. That is why some words look wrong: <em>an hour</em> (the h is silent), <em>an honest man</em>, but <em>a house</em> (the h is pronounced).</p><p>Some words start with the letter u or eu but sound like <em>you</em>. That is a consonant sound, so we say <em>a university</em>, <em>a uniform</em>, <em>a European city</em>. Compare <em>an umbrella</em> (u sounds like uh).</p><p>If you are not sure, say the word aloud and listen to the first sound.</p>"
        }
      }
